.TH vellum "" "Command"
.SH NAME
vellum \- GUI technical drawing and schematic editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvellum [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-print\fR|\fB\-pic\fR|\fB\-net\fR|\fB\-hpgl\fR|\fB\-bom\fR|\fB\-check\fR|\fB\-ps\fR|\fB\-dxf\fR \
[\fB\-wide\fR] [\fB\-fit\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-renum\fR [\fB\-base\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d\fR > \fIout.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
vellum -- GUI technical drawing and schematic editor
Usage:	vellum [ options ] [ file ]
	vellum -print|-pic|-net|-hpgl|-bom|-check|-ps|-dxf
		[-wide] [-fit] [-scale n] file.d ...
	vellum -renum [-base n] file.d > out.d
Keys:	r rotate, m mirror, d duplicate, n properties, x/Del delete,
	u undo, v zoom-to-fit, f to front, j/J group/ungroup,
	* / scale selection x2 / half,
	1-6 align L/R/T/B + distribute, g grid, < > sheets, Esc select,
	F2 save, F3 open, F4 new, F5-F7 cut/copy/paste, F8 settings
.HE
.B vellum
is the drawing board of this workstation: electronic schematics,
block diagrams, flowcharts, network maps, software structure charts
and figures for manuals, with clean printed output and full
membership in the Unix pipeline.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop and accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
The optional
.I file
argument names a drawing to open.
.PP
The file name sits in its own bar at the top, with a `*' while the
drawing has unsaved changes; at the bar's right edge the
.B <
and
.B >
cells walk a numbered sheet set \- they sit on the file line because
the sheet is a property of the FILE (the same actions as the
.B <
and
.B >
keys; see SHEET SETS).
The TOOLBAR row of cells sits under it, and the palette down the
left edge holds the symbol bank.
Symbols come from LIBRARY FILES named in /usr/vellum/etc/libs:
.B discrete
(resistor through opamp),
.B logic
(gates and flip-flops),
.B flow
(flowchart stencils),
.B net
(network-map stencils),
.B arch
(software structure stencils),
.B pid
(process / P&ID stencils),
.B power
(one-line electrical) and
.B plan
(floor-plan stencils), plus the user scratch library
/usr/vellum/etc/symbols when it holds anything.
Every loaded symbol stays resolvable at once \- a drawing may mix
libraries \- but the palette shows ONE group at a time: the header
cell above the bank names it and clicking the header switches to the
next.
After the last library comes the
.B shapes
pseudo-group: the nine STRETCHABLE shapes (box, rounded box,
diamond, oval, parallelogram, drum, document, circle, ellipse), the
four CONNECTOR styles (hv, line, arrow, harrow), the Arc tool, the
Net name marker and the Dim (dimension) tool.
The toolbar's
.B Lib
cell lists the libraries on file in a scrollable dialog, and
.B Edit
opens the current library in the
.B symedit
symbol editor.
.PP
The tools:
.DS
.ta 8n 16n
	\fBSel\fR	click selects (a grouped object selects its whole
		group), drag moves; a drag on EMPTY canvas selects
		everything inside the rectangle.  A selected SHAPE
		shows corner handles \- dragging one resizes it \-
		and a selected line, wire, connector or dimension
		shows ENDPOINT grips (a polyline its vertices):
		dragging one re-routes and re-snaps the end
	\fBWire\fR	drag or click each end; wires route H-then-V, snap
		to pins and re-anchor so a run chains corner by
		corner \- \fBEsc\fR or a click back on the anchor
		ends the run
	\fBLine\fR	like Wire but straight \- and a chained run folds
		into ONE polyline object
	\fBBox\fR	drag draws a rectangle
	\fBCirc\fR	drag from the centre draws a circle
	\fBText\fR	click asks for a label and places it
	\fBDel\fR	click deletes what it hits
.DE
Between the tool groups the
.B Grid
cell toggles the grid dots; then the ACTION cells
.B Rot ,
.B Mir ,
.B Name
and
.B Dup
arm click-to-apply versions of the keys;
.B In
and
.B Out
zoom (also
.B +
and
.B \- ,
and
.B v
zooms to fit the whole drawing);
.B Fr
and
.B Bk
move the selection to the front or back of the stacking order; and
the
.B L\fIn\fR
cell cycles the ACTIVE layer new objects land on.
.PP
A SHAPE is placed by dragging its rectangle, then typing the label
(drawn centred, clipped to the shape); shapes expose ATTACHMENT
POINTS at their edge midpoints and corners.
A CONNECTOR is drawn with the wire gestures but snaps to attachment
points and symbol pins, and STAYS ATTACHED: moving or resizing the
target re-routes the connector.
The
.B arrow
and
.B harrow
styles carry an arrowhead at the far end.
The Arc cell draws a circular arc in two stages: drag from the
centre to the start point, then click where the arc ends.
The Net cell places a named net marker for the netlist export.
.PP
The
.B Dim
cell draws a DIMENSION point to point with the wire gestures; both
ends snap to attachment points, so dimensioning a shape's edge is
two clicks.
It lands on the annotation layer, with extension ticks, arrowheads
both ends and a centred label.
An EMPTY label is automatic: the measured distance times the sheet
unit, integer formatted (``85 mm''); any text set through the style
dialog overrides it, and emptying the text goes back to automatic.
The sheet unit (``one grid unit is 5 mm'') is set in Settings and
saved in the drawing; coordinates stay grid units \- only labels
multiply.
A LEADER needs no tool of its own: an
.B arrow
connector plus a text label already is one.
.PP
The
.B n
key (or the Name tool) edits a symbol's designator and value; on any
other object it opens the STYLE dialog: solid/dashed/dotted line
style, white/gray/black fill (fills stack in z-order), bold (width
2),
.B Hatch
(a gray fill renders as 45-degree hatching \- cross-section shading),
the object's layer, its text and \- for a text label \- one of
three sizes and
.B Vert ,
which turns the label 90 degrees (axis labels, vertical dimensions).
A text label may hold several lines separated by `|'.
A polyline checked
.B Smooth
renders as a spline through its points, and
.B \-pic
exports it as one.
A FILLED polyline is a closed even-odd fill over its points \- the
arbitrary solid shape mechanical sketches want \- on screen, in
every export and in the preview.
.SH LAYERS
Four fixed layers: 0
.B drawing
(the default), 1
.B annotation ,
2
.B frame
(title block, sheet border) and 3
.B construction ,
which never prints.
The Settings dialog (window menu) checks which layers are VISIBLE
and which PRINT, picks the snap pitch (1 or 2 units) and the sheet
preset (160x120, or A4/A3/A2 at 8 dots per unit \- now that pages
can leave on a laser or a plotter, the screenful is not the natural
page for every drawing), and its
.B Frame
button stamps the TEMPLATE /usr/vellum/etc/frame.d \- ordinary
drawing lines fed through the parser onto the frame layer, with
.BR $F " " $D " " $S " " $R
substituted in text values (file name, date, ``Sheet n/m'', revision)
\- so a company title block is an edit to that file, not a rebuild.
The sheet edge shows as a dotted construction guide.
.SH EDITING
.DS
.ta 6n 22n
	\fBr\fR	rotate a quarter turn (ANY selection turns about
		its centre; a lone symbol turns in place)
	\fBm\fR	mirror (likewise: any selection)
	\fBd\fR	duplicate, two units down-right
	\fBn\fR	properties (name/value or style dialog)
	\fBx\fR, \fBDel\fR	delete the selection
	\fBu\fR	UNDO the last commit (once more redoes)
	\fBv\fR	zoom to fit the drawing
	\fBf\fR	selection to the front (stacking)
	\fBj\fR, \fBJ\fR	group / ungroup the selection
	\fB*\fR, \fB/\fR	scale the selection: double / halve about
		its corner (halving rounds odd coordinates
		and the status line says so)
	\fB1 2 3 4\fR	align left / right / top / bottom
	\fB5 6\fR	distribute evenly, horizontally / vertically
	\fBs w l b c t e\fR	arm Sel/Wire/Line/Box/Circ/Text/Del
	\fBg\fR	toggle the grid dots (also the Grid cell)
	\fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR	zoom in / out
	\fB<\fR, \fB>\fR	previous / next sheet of a numbered set
	\fBEsc\fR	cancel the armed tool, placement or run;
		a second \fBEsc\fR drops the selection
	\fBF2 F3 F4\fR	Save / Open / New (as in \fBzedit\fR)
	\fBF5 F6 F7\fR	Cut / Copy / Paste
	\fBF8\fR	the Settings dialog
	\fBF11\fR	Help: this page, opened in \fBzman\fR
		(also the window menu's Help entry)
	arrows	pan; \fBClear/Home\fR pans to the origin
.DE
The window menu's
.B Print
entry opens the print dialog: scale, wide, and the three verbs \-
.B Print
spools the drawing through velxport into
.BR lpr ,
.B Preview
opens a window (velprev) drawing the printable extent through the
SAME renderer that feeds the printer, so what it shows is what the
paper gets.
The
.B Find
entry searches designators, values, text and net names for a
case-blind substring, selects the hit and pans it to centre \- Find
again steps to the next hit; its
.B Sheets
button lists the numbered set and jumps by number.
The
.B Dup
toolbar cell with a live selection opens the ARRAY dialog (columns,
rows, pitch) \- pin headers and terminal strips in one commit; the
armed Dup tool still copies single objects click by click.
While a wire, line, connector or dimension drag is up, the status
line's coordinate cell shows the run's LENGTH times the sheet unit \-
measuring without committing a dimension.
Zoom reaches down to 2 px per unit \- the whole A4 sheet in one
window \- where the grid thins to every fourth unit.
A text, shape label or dimension override may run to 39 characters;
the value of a symbol stays 15.
Cut, Copy and Paste (window menu) go through the system clipboard AS
TEXT in the drawing format \- paste a fragment into another Vellum,
or into
.B zedit
as text.
A middle CLICK pastes at the pointer (the selection, else the
clipboard); a middle DRAG pans the sheet.
Persistent groups select, move, copy and delete as one until
ungrouped.
When the drawing is modified, an autosave writes
.IB file .bak
every five minutes.
.SH "SHEET SETS"
A sheet is a file; a drawing that spans sheets is a SET of numbered
files
.RI ( amp1.d ,
.IR amp2.d ,
\&...).
The
.B >
key saves the current sheet and loads the next \- past the last one
it offers to create it, which is how a set grows \- and
.B <
walks back.
The Frame stamp adds ``Sheet n/m'' when the file name carries a
number.
Shell globs are the set syntax for the exporters:
.B "vellum \-net amp?.d"
computes nets per sheet and MERGES them BY NAME \- a net named (by an
N marker or GND/VCC) VBUS on sheet 1 is the same conductor as VBUS on
sheet 3; unnamed nets stay sheet-local and print qualified
(NET\-2.3 = sheet 2, net 3).
.B \-print
emits the form feed between sheets, and
.B \-bom
yields ONE parts list for the whole set.
An off-page reference is the OFFP stencil with its value reading
``\(->3'' and an N net name on its pin \- the netlist merge makes it
electrically true.
.PP
A BUS is an idiom, not an object: draw the trunk as a BOLD wire \-
graphics, not electricity \- and tap it with short wires carrying
.B "N D0"
\&...
.BR "N D7" ;
same-named taps merge across the whole sheet set, which is what a
bus MEANS.
.B \-check
flags a net name appearing on fewer than two pins in the set \- the
typo'd bit (D3 on one end, D8 on the other) that eyes cannot see.
.SH "HEADLESS EXPORT"
The export modes run WITHOUT a window \- from make(1), from
scripts, even on a machine with no graphics card (they exec the
helper /usr/vellum/lib/velxport); each takes ONE OR MORE sheet
files:
.DS
.ta 4n 28n
	\fBvellum -print\fR f.d	Epson raster on stdout (ESC * bands,
		1 grid unit = 8 dots; pipe to \fBlpr\fR).
		\fB-wide\fR prints landscape (the page turned a
		quarter), \fB-scale\fR \fIn\fR picks n dots per
		grid unit \- small drawings print big
	\fBvellum -pic\fR f.d	troff pic source on stdout (16 units
		per inch), for figures in manuals; smooth
		polylines come out as pic splines
	\fBvellum -hpgl\fR f.d	HPGL pen-plotter commands on stdout;
		the annotation layer draws in the SECOND
		pen (SP2) \- the cheapest two-color print
		this machine will ever do
	\fBvellum -ps\fR f.d ...	PostScript (Level 1) on stdout, one
		page per sheet \- the laser's page.  Text
		is Courier sized so the monospace advance
		matches the editor cell: nothing collides
		on paper that did not collide on screen.
		Smooth polylines emit real curveto,
		vertical text rotates, arcs are true arcs.
		\fB-fit\fR scales the drawing to fill the
		page (for \fB-print\fR it picks the widest
		step of the discrete 2..32 ladder instead)
	\fBvellum -dxf\fR f.d ...	DXF (R10 entities) on stdout, for
		the office CAD: coordinates in grid units,
		arcs as true ARC entities, our layer
		number as the DXF layer name.  \fBveldxf\fR
		converts the other way, and the pair
		fix-points
	\fBvellum -renum\fR f.d	canonical designators: symbols
		sorted by (y, x), numbered per prefix from
		\fB-base\fR \fIn\fR (default 1), the drawing
		re-emitted on stdout.  One sheet per run; a
		set renumbers sheet by sheet with -base
		carrying the count across
	\fBvellum -net\fR f.d ...	netlist: one line per net, R1.2-style
		pins (named pins print Q1.B), GND/VCC and
		N markers name nets \- MERGED by name across
		the sheet set; designator-less stencils
		(rails, taps, off-page markers) carry nets
		but are never listed; warnings for single-
		pin nets and unconnected pins on stderr
	\fBvellum -bom\fR f.d ...	bill of materials over the whole
		set: count, stencil, value and the
		designators, sorted
	\fBvellum -check\fR f.d ...	prove the set: duplicate
		designators, dangling wire ends, parts with
		no value, symbol codes no library defines
		(lines the loader would DROP), sheets whose
		U units disagree, single-pin nets and
		unconnected pins \- plus the ERC: between
		TYPED pins (see \fBsymedit\fR), two outputs
		on a net conflict, an output must not drive
		a power rail, and a net whose typed pins
		are all inputs is undriven.  An untyped
		library produces silence, not noise.  The
		conductor checks
		(dangling ends, nets, pins) apply only to
		sheets that contain WIRES \- a diagram
		sheet's stencil pins are attachment
		points, not terminals.  One finding per
		line on stdout; the EXIT STATUS is the
		finding count, so make(1) gates on it:
		  check: ; vellum -check amp?.d
		  print: check ; vellum -print amp?.d | lpr
.DE
Layers marked no-print and the construction layer are skipped.
.SH "FILE FORMAT"
Drawings are plain text, one object per line, in grid units; unknown
lines are skipped, so old readers accept new files:
.DS
.ta 8n
	vellum1
	U 5 mm
	Y NPN 20 30 0 0 Q1 -
	W 10 10 20 10
	L 5 5 9 9 /1.0
	T 12 4 s1 Power supply
	S box 60 10 75 20 Fetch
	K harrow 67 20 67 24 @67,20 -
	P 3 5 50 10 55 5 60
	A 30 50 4 0 180
	N 17 10 OUT
	D 10 40 24 40
	G 2
.DE
.B Y
symbol,
.B W
wire,
.B L
line,
.B B
box,
.B C
circle,
.B T
text (with an
.BI s n
size),
.B S
a stretchable shape,
.B K
a connector whose ends are `\-' (free) or
.BI @ x,y
(the attachment's grid point, re-resolved on load),
.B P
a polyline,
.B A
an arc (centre, radius, angles),
.B N
a net name,
.B D
a dimension (empty text = the automatic label),
.B U
the sheet-units header and
.B G
a group header covering the following n objects.
An optional
.BI / flags . layer
token carries line style, fill, bold, hatch, smooth, vertical text
and the layer.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 4n 30n
	/usr/vellum/lib/*.sym	the symbol libraries
	/usr/vellum/etc/libs	which of them load at start-up
	/usr/vellum/etc/symbols	the user scratch library
	/usr/vellum/etc/frame.d	the Frame stamp's template
	/usr/vellum/etc/project.mk	the sheet-set Makefile skeleton:
		cp it next to the sheets and set SHEETS \-
		check: gates print:, ps: and bom:
	/usr/vellum/eg/*.d	SAMPLE drawings, one per domain:
		amp logic (schematics), flow lan struct
		(diagrams), bracket (mechanical, with
		dimensions), feed oneline office (P&ID,
		one-line, floor plan), psu1 psu2 (a two-
		sheet set for -net merging), curve
		(a velgraph plot, annotated), plate
		(imported from plate.dxf, kept beside
		it).  Open one, or feed them to the
		exporters, to see every feature working.
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B symedit ,
.B veldxf ,
.B velgraph ,
.B zview ,
.B zman ,
.B lpr
.SH NOTES
Wires connect only through junction dots; connectors never dot.
One level of undo only; the printed page and the pic figure are the
first-class outputs.
